"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Preoperative PSA was elevated at 8.3. The surgical findings included a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2), positive surgical margins, and the absence of seminal vesicle invasion and lymphovascular invasion. The absence of lymph node removal and the lack of documented nodal sampling introduce uncertainty, as pNx is not equivalent to pN0. The biopsy grade (Gleason 3+3, ISUP grade group 1) differs from the prostatectomy specimen grade. Countervailing factors include the absence of lymphovascular invasion and seminal vesicle invasion. Information that would revise the prognosis significantly is the postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory. Missing postoperative PSA or follow-up data prevents a definitive assessment of recurrence risk. The current heuristic horizon suggests a potential recurrence window. The fixed estimated horizon is 84.42 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 54.87–113.97 months; its coverage has not been validated."
